---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import RelatedArticles from '../components/shared/RelatedArticles.astro';
import MidContentAd from '../components/shared/MidContentAd.astro';
import EndOfContentAd from '../components/shared/EndOfContentAd.astro';
import Breadcrumbs from '../components/shared/Breadcrumbs.astro';
import EditorialStandards from '../components/shared/EditorialStandards.astro';

const SITE = 'https://vitamindcalculator.net';
const PAGE_URL = `${SITE}/vitamin-d-and-vitiligo`;

const medicalJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'MedicalWebPage',
  name: 'Vitamin D and Vitiligo',
  url: PAGE_URL,
  medicalAudience: { '@type': 'MedicalAudience', audienceType: 'Patient' },
  about: [{ '@type': 'MedicalCondition', name: 'Vitiligo', code: { '@type': 'MedicalCode', codeValue: 'L80', codingSystem: 'ICD-10' } }],
  lastReviewed: '2026-05-01',
};
const breadcrumbJsonLd = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
  itemListElement: [
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: SITE },
    { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Vitamin D and Vitiligo', item: PAGE_URL },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Vitamin D and Vitiligo: Topical Calcipotriene, Oral Adjunct, and Narrowband UVB"
  description="What the evidence shows for topical calcipotriene, oral vitamin D repletion, and how narrowband UVB works in vitiligo — both directly and via vitamin D synthesis."
  ogType="article"
>
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(medicalJsonLd)} />
  <script slot="head" type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(breadcrumbJsonLd)} />

  <Breadcrumbs crumbs={[{ href: "/vitamin-d-and-vitiligo", label: "Vitamin D & Vitiligo" }]} />
  <EditorialStandards />

  <main class="max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 py-10">
    <h1 class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Vitamin D and Vitiligo</h1>
    <p class="text-lg text-gray-600 leading-relaxed mb-8">
      Vitiligo is an autoimmune loss of melanocytes causing depigmented skin patches.
      Vitamin D links to vitiligo on three fronts: topical vitamin D analogues as
      adjunctive therapy, oral repletion for the frequent underlying deficiency, and
      narrowband UVB phototherapy which itself raises 25(OH)D.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mb-3">Deficiency prevalence</h2>
    <MidContentAd />
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Multiple studies find lower mean 25(OH)D in vitiligo patients than controls, and
      higher rates of deficiency. The 2015 Iranian study by Aghaei found 68% of vitiligo
      patients had 25(OH)D &lt; 20 ng/mL vs 40% of controls. Vitiligo shares associations
      with other autoimmune conditions (thyroid disease, alopecia areata) where vitamin D
      deficiency is also common.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Topical calcipotriene / calcipotriol</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Calcipotriene is a synthetic vitamin D analogue. Used alone it produces modest
      repigmentation in vitiligo, but combining it with narrowband UVB (Kumaran 2006)
      or topical steroids (Newman 2016) improves repigmentation rates over either alone.
      Common practice in dermatology for stable, localised vitiligo.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Narrowband UVB (311 nm)</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      NB-UVB is a first-line phototherapy for generalised vitiligo, with 60–70% of
      patients achieving at least moderate repigmentation over 6–12 months. It works via
      direct melanocyte stimulation and immunomodulation — the vitamin D synthesis is a
      bonus but not the primary mechanism. Some dermatologists check pre-treatment
      25(OH)D and supplement deficient patients to potentiate response.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Oral vitamin D supplementation</h2>
    <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
      Small trials of high-dose oral vitamin D (35,000 IU/day for 6 months, Finamor
      2013) reported repigmentation in some previously refractory patients. Such doses
      require calcium monitoring and are not standard care. Correcting deficiency to
      standard target (≥ 30 ng/mL) is a reasonable adjunct without controversy; going
      to hypertherapeutic doses is experimental.
    </p>

    <h2 class="text-2xl font-bold text-gray-900 mt-8 mb-3">Practical guide</h2>
    <ol class="list-decimal pl-6 text-gray-700 leading-relaxed space-y-2">
      <li>Test 25(OH)D at diagnosis — vitiligo patients frequently deficient.</li>
      <li>Correct to ≥ 30 ng/mL with 2,000–4,000 IU/day cholecalciferol.</li>
      <li>Combine with dermatologist-directed therapy — topical calcipotriene ± steroids, NB-UVB, JAK inhibitors (ruxolitinib cream approved 2022 for vitiligo).</li>
      <li>Do not use high-dose oral vitamin D as a first-line vitiligo therapy without dermatology involvement.</li>
    </ol>

    <EndOfContentAd />

    <div class="bg-blue-50 border border-blue-200 rounded-xl p-4 text-sm text-blue-900 mt-8 leading-relaxed">
      <strong>Not medical advice.</strong> Sudden or rapidly spreading depigmentation
      warrants prompt dermatology evaluation — early treatment offers better
      repigmentation prospects than delayed treatment.
    </div>

    <RelatedArticles current="/vitamin-d-and-vitiligo" />
  </main>
</BaseLayout>
